;; The public health route's DECISION CORE — whether this request is the
;; process-liveness probe.
;;
;; The JVM still owns the socket. amu's http-ingress kit is host-listen,
;; guest-poll, and its `:native-aot` is pending; this file does not pretend
;; otherwise (ADR-0038). What moves here is the judgement the handler used to
;; write as two string equals: GET and `/health`. The JSON body is not a
;; decision and stays a host constant.
;;
;; `health.cljc` CALLS this file through `cloud.itonami.app.kotoba-oracle`,
;; from the shipped `resources/cloud/itonami/app/oracle/health.kir.edn`. There
;; is one implementation. The host still names the path in `server.clj` so the
;; route scanner can see it; Kotoba is the admission of that named route. A
;; handler that kept the two equals and dropped this call would still 200, so
;; `health-http-test` inverts the artifact and requires `/health` to stop
;; answering.
;;
;; ## The native slice
;;
;; Two `:string` arguments and a `:bool` result. That is the native-crossable
;; surface (`:i64` / `:bool` / `:string`); a record is not required. `and`
;; desugars to nested `let`/`if` and compiles on both native ISAs — measured
;; on `policy.kotoba`. Do not add a self-restriction here without compiling
;; for `:x86_64-kotoba-v1` first.
;;
;; Temporary spelling, as everywhere in this migration (ADR-2608650000): none
;; beyond staying inside the word-typed slice. Removal condition: none of
;; this is a workaround for a missing construct.

(ns cloud.itonami.app.health
  (:export [health-route?]))

(defn health-route? [method :string path :string] :bool
  (and (string=? method "GET")
       (string=? path "/health")))
